  "describe-item": "Write the object.\n\nCity: {{cityName}}\nTheme: {{theme}}\nWhat it physically is: {{archetype}}\n\nGive it the name someone here would actually use for it, and a line saying what\nit is made of and what this city has done to it. It can be ordinary. Do not make\nit magical or valuable unless the theme calls for it.\n\nNames already spoken for in this city, which this one must not repeat:\n\n{{usedNames}}\n",
  "describe-npc": "Write the person who works or spends their time here.\n\nCity: {{cityName}}\nTheme: {{theme}}\nWhat the town is about:\n{{premise}}\nPlace: {{placeName}}, a {{placeLabel}}\n{{charter}}\nTheir role there: {{role}}\n\nGive them a name, the way they behave when a stranger walks in, the things they\nknow, their own life, and the facts about them a player earns.\n\nThe name is an ordinary one: a first name and a family name of the kind real\npeople have, that another person can spell and say after hearing it once.\nNothing invented, no coined syllables, no spelling chosen to look unusual, no\nflourish.\n\nTheir family name starts with one of these letters: {{letters}}.\n\nFor the behaviour, take the one habit somebody would notice from the doorway and\nsay what it looks like. It is what the player meets, not a life story and not a\nlist of adjectives.\n\nWhat they know is what they could have overheard or noticed from this spot: who\nowes what, who was in last night, what is late, what is broken, what the weather\ndid to the work. Tie it to {{cityName}} and to {{placeName}} by name, to the\npeople already living here and to the trade this city runs on, so it could not\nhave been said in any other town.\n\n## Their life\n\nEvery field of `life` is theirs alone, and two people in one room answer\ndifferently because of it:\n\n- `reason`: why they are at this spot at this hour, first person, a sentence\n  they could say out loud to a stranger who asked.\n- `errand`: what they are doing when they are out walking, first person, said\n  the same way, naming no place: where they are going is decided elsewhere.\n- `history`: where they came from, what they did before, and what the town's\n  own turn did to them, using the words of the town's story.\n- `interests`, `manner`, `cares`, `avoids`: what they talk about given the\n  chance, how they speak, what they will stand up for, and what they will not\n  be drawn on.\n\n## What the player earns\n\n`background` is the codex: staged facts, each with what earns it. Use every\nstage: `met` for what is plain from seeing them at their post, `talked` for\ntheir history a sentence at a time and the thing they like talking about,\n`quest` for what they care about, which only somebody who did their job learns,\nand `told` for the one thing about them that is only ever heard from somebody\nelse.\n\nNames already spoken for in this city, which this person must not repeat or echo\nin either half of their name:\n\n{{usedNames}}\n",
  "name-city": "Name the city.\n\nTheme: {{theme}}\n\nWhat the city is:\n\n{{premise}}\n\nGive it the kind of name painted on the road sign at the edge of town: one\npeople say quickly, that sounds like it has been there a while and that suits\nthe trade the place lives off. Build it out of everyday words and ordinary\nnames, so a stranger can spell it after hearing it once. Do not explain the\nname.\n",
  "name-districts": "Name the parts of {{cityName}}, all at once. These are the districts: what the\nmap labels, what somebody says instead of a bearing, and what a stranger says\nthey are from.\n\nTheme: {{theme}}\nWhat the town is about:\n{{premise}}\n\n## The parts\n\nOne name for each, carrying the label it was given:\n\n{{districts}}\n\n## How a district is named\n\nA district name is short and said out loud. Two or three words at most, and one\nor two is better. Mix these:\n\n- [thing the town is built on] [place word]\n- [side of town] [place word]\n- [one word] and a place word run together as a single word\n- The [one word]\n\nA place word is the plain noun a part of town ends in: bay, end, row, gate,\nside, reach, bank, hill, quarter, basin, cross, green.\n\nThe square brackets are slots for your own words. Take them from what this town\nlives on, what happened to it and what it is arguing about, so the names sound\nlike this town and nowhere else. A name may carry the side of town it is on\nwhere that reads naturally, and a part in the middle of town is named after\nsomething else.\n\nEvery part of the name is an everyday word or an ordinary family name: nothing\ninvented and nothing spelled to look unusual.\n\nNever the words district, quarter, zone or sector with a number after them, and\nnever a name that is only a compass point.\n\nNo two parts of this city are called the same thing.\n",
  "name-place": "Name this building.\n\nCity: {{cityName}}\nTheme: {{theme}}\nWhat the town is about:\n{{premise}}\nKind of building: {{label}}\n{{charter}}\n\nName it the way its owner would have named it. A bar and an office are named\nvery differently: one is a sign hung over a door, the other is a family name and\na trade. Write a name a signwriter would actually paint: everyday words and\nordinary names, nothing invented or fanciful. Do not put the kind of building in\nthe name unless that is really how such places are named around here.\n\nNo word may head two signs in this city, the head being the first word after\nany \"The\". Names already spoken for here, which this one must not repeat, echo,\nor share a head word with:\n\n{{usedNames}}\n",
  "name-signs": "Name these buildings, all at once. What the player gets of each of them is a\ndoor, a sign and a frontage read from the street.\n\nCity: {{cityName}}\nTheme: {{theme}}\nWhat the town is about:\n{{premise}}\n\n## What these buildings are\n\n{{kinds}}\n\n## The buildings\n\nOne name for each, carrying the label it was given:\n\n{{buildings}}\n\n## How a sign is written\n\nA street reads as real because its signs are not all one shape. Mix these, and\nlet the trade, the street and the town's own story decide which fits:\n\n- [first name]'s [trade], or just [first name]'s\n- [family name] & [sons, daughters, partners], or [family name] [trade]\n- [place word] [trade], with the trade said plainly\n- [number] [street]\n- The [adjective] [noun]\n\nThe square brackets are slots for your own words. Use the town's trades, its\nstreets, its weather and its argument, so the signs sound like they were painted\nhere and nowhere else. A first name or a family name in a slot is an ordinary\none, the kind real people carry and a signwriter can paint without asking how it\nis spelled; every other word is an everyday word, nothing invented. Do not put the kind of building in the name unless that\nis really how such places are named around here.\n\nNo word may head two signs in this city. The head is the first word after any\n\"The\": two signs starting with the same family name, the same adjective or the\nsame number read as one place twice. Within this batch, every head is\ndifferent.\n\nWords already heading a sign in this city, which no sign here may start with:\n\n{{usedHeads}}\n",
