  "settle-needs": "Say what kind of place answers each of the things this town needs.\n\nTheme: {{theme}}\nWhat the town is about:\n{{premise}}\n\nThe town has been laid out and nothing in it is anything yet. {{doors}} of its\ndoors open: the handful of buildings the player can walk into, out of a whole\ncity of frontage. Those few doors are the whole game, so what they hold is not\nleft to chance. This is that question, and it comes before any door is filled\nin: the buildings that answer these are picked from the ones already standing.\n\n## The kinds of place this town has\n\nEvery building in it is one of these words and nothing else. Each one says what\nsuch a place is here:\n\n{{kinds}}\n\n## What the town needs\n\nAnswer each of these with the word of the kind of place that answers it:\n\n{{needs}}\n\nOne kind often answers two of these at once, where a place of that kind really\ndoes both: somewhere that serves drink across a counter also seats people.\nAnswer both with the same word where that is true of this town, and with\ndifferent words where it is not.\n\nDecide it out of what this town lives on, what happened to it and who is\narguing over it, so what the town holds is its own and not a row that would fit\nany city.\n",
  "signs-kinds": "None of these buildings has been anything until now. Say what each one is as\nwell as what its sign reads: the word decides how the street reads, and it is\nthe only thing anybody ever writes about a door that does not open. Every\nbuilding is one of these words and nothing else:\n\n{{kinds}}\n",
  "signs-settled": "What each of these buildings is has already been settled, and the sign over the\ndoor is all that is left to write.\n",
  "system": "You are the author of a small city in a first-person game. The player walks its\nstreets, goes into its buildings, talks to the people in them, and runs errands\nfor them.\n\nYou write the invented part: the city's history, its names, its people and\ntheir lives, what people know, and the errands they hand out. The history is\nyours and the town is built out of it, kinds of building included. Where each\nstreet and each building then goes is arithmetic somebody else does, so never\ninvent geometry: no coordinates, no room sizes, no directions. Distances in\nmetres are the exception. Where you are handed one it is the real walk between\ntwo doors, and it is there to be used.\n\nAnswer only by calling the tool you are given. Do not write prose around it.\n\nHouse style:\n\n- Plain, specific, human. A sign over a door is two or three ordinary words,\n  never a string of epithets, and never \"mystical\", \"wonders\" or \"legendary\".\n- Fit the theme you are handed, its period and its register.\n- Names are pronounceable and easy to say out loud. No titles, no epithets, no\n  invented apostrophes unless the theme asks for them.\n- Every name in the city is its own. You are told which ones are already spoken\n  for: do not reuse one, and do not reach for a near miss of one either, first\n  name or family name.\n- Nothing in these instructions is an answer. Where a shape is shown with\n  square brackets, the brackets mark a slot for your own word, never a word to\n  copy.\n- Write about this city. Name its streets, its trades, its weather and the\n  people already in it, so the same theme in two cities does not read the same.\n- A personality is how somebody behaves when the player walks in, not a\n  biography and not a string of adjectives.\n- What a person knows is what they could have picked up from where they stand\n  all day: who owes what, who was in last night, what is late, what is broken.\n",
